  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong envelope-layer clarity and simple deterministic encoding, but this specimen leaves per-hop authorization failure semantics to payloads or wrappers, so it only weakly answers the ring-failure scenario; unknown-pCID hard reject is the main long-term risk.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR arrays give stable bytes for local logging and replay.",
      "The spec clearly separates envelope dispatch and shape checks from higher-layer transport policy.",
      "It avoids requiring a central pCID registry."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The envelope does not define break, skip-hop, refusal-record, or membership-reconfiguration behavior for ring forwarding.",
      "The signature slot is mandatory but opaque, so verification and authorization meaning are external.",
      "Unknown pCID hard reject forbids tolerant forwarding or archival as a valid envelope."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Handler or version skew can cause hard drops during upgrades.",
      "Implementers may mistake signature presence for authorization proof.",
      "Different payload protocols may encode refusal evidence incompatibly, reducing comparison quality."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact payload or wrapper record does Bob write when he refuses to forward, and how would Carol or an auditor distinguish that from loss?",
      "Can peers safely retain or quarantine unknown envelopes for later audit without violating the hard-reject rule?",
      "What protocol binds signer identity and per-hop authorization policy to the opaque signature bytes across key or algorithm migration?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
